# ===========================================================================
# WHAT DOES NOT GO INTO VERSION CONTROL
# Land Intelligence Platform - Geocode Spatial Solutions Ltd
#
# The repository holds the things that took twelve sessions to write and
# cannot be re-downloaded: the ETL scripts, the enrichment engine, the
# verifiers, the migrations, the delivery layer, and the documents that are
# the durable record.
#
# It does NOT hold data that can be fetched again, anything large enough to
# make the history unusable, or the database password.
# ===========================================================================

# --- THE PASSWORD. This line matters more than the rest of the file. ------
# 03_etl/.env holds DB_PASSWORD in plain text. Every script reads it from
# there. A commit that includes it publishes it, and rewriting history does
# not un-publish anything - once pushed, treat the password as burned and
# change it. .env.example is the template and IS tracked.
.env
*.env
!.env.example

# --- Re-downloadable. Large. Not ours. ------------------------------------
# 06_rasters is the raster corpus - CHIRPS, SoilGrids, WorldCover, GHSL,
# VIIRS, the DEM. Tens of gigabytes, all of it public and all of it fetchable
# again. The recovery shopping list is the .rasters.csv written beside every
# backup by backup_01_full.ps1; that is what makes re-fetching possible, not
# storing the files here.
06_rasters/
03_etl/data/

# --- Machine-local, rebuildable in one command ----------------------------
03_etl/venv/
**/__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo

# --- Database dumps. Backups, not source. ---------------------------------
# These belong on an external drive and in cloud storage - not in a git
# history, where they would bloat every clone forever.
*.dump
*.sql.gz
*.backup
geocode-backups/

# --- Generated reports ----------------------------------------------------
# 06_delivery/out holds PDFs produced by pdf_01_report.py. They are OUTPUT -
# rebuildable from the database in one command - and several of them are a
# real client's plots. Generated deliverables carrying client data do not
# belong in a source history, where they are permanent and travel with every
# clone.
#
# The first commit included twenty of them. To untrack without deleting:
#     git rm -r --cached 06_delivery/out
06_delivery/out/

# --- Editor and OS noise --------------------------------------------------
.vscode/
.idea/
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*~

# --- QGIS working files ---------------------------------------------------
# The project files themselves are worth keeping; their autosave and lock
# files are not.
*.qgz~
*.qgs~
*~.qgs
.qgis2/

# ===========================================================================
# DELIBERATELY TRACKED, in case someone is tempted to add them here
#
#   *.bak                 the ETL .bak files are the ONLY history this
#                         project has ever had. Once git exists they become
#                         redundant, but they are small text and they are
#                         evidence. Keep them until the history is deep
#                         enough to replace them.
#
#   _live_schema_snapshot.sql
#                         197 KB of text and the whole point of the E10
#                         check. Its value is entirely in being diffable
#                         against the previous version, which is what a
#                         repository is for.
#
#   02_data_catalogue/datasets.csv
#                         every source, licence and ETL status. This is the
#                         licence position in one file - the thing an
#                         acquirer asks for.
#
#   04_docs/correspondence/
#                         the five drafted letters. What was asked of whom
#                         and when is a legal record.
# ===========================================================================
